model.en:
canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
  const required = this.reflector.getAllAndOverride<string[]>(ROLES_KEY, [
    context.getHandler(),
    context.getClass(),
  ]);
  if (!required) return true; // no @Roles() on this route -> open
  const { user } = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
  return required.some((role) => user?.roles?.includes(role));
}

Why: the guard reads the route metadata via the Reflector, using getAllAndOverride so a handler-level @Roles() overrides a controller default. It returns true/false (or you can throw ForbiddenException for a 403 with a message, which your exception filter then renders). Because the guard runs after middleware and before any pipe or interceptor, authorization is settled before the body is even validated, and the check is now reusable and bindable at method/controller/global scope.

